# Website mirrors of backend policy

The website may re-state a backend rule to build copy, disable a control, or pre-validate a field. It may never own that rule.

## Required

- One module per rule family, next to its consumers. Pure mirrors export constants + predicates together when callers are non-hook code. Shipped: `customer/meetings/meetingScheduleLead.ts`, `customer/meetings/meetingNotifyTemplateMode.ts` (when present). Attend window: `components/meeting/hooks/useMeetingAttendWindow.ts` — session calls it **once** and exposes `attendWindow`; screens read from session (do not call the hook again). Mirror math stays private.
- Every mirrored constant names its backend source in a one-line comment (`Mirror of backend MeetingModel.MIN_LEAD_MS — keep in sync.` / `MeetingModel.ATTEND_OPEN_BEFORE_MS`).
- Derived predicates live in the mirror module too, so a rule change is one edit: `countVotingParticipants` is used by the details roster **and** `useCustomerHome`, not re-filtered per screen. Attend-window consumers must not re-copy the 30-minute math.
- The server keeps the enforcing gate (Joi rule, Ability, requester) for write-path schedule rules. Live attend-window / `enterLive` are **client session gates** today — copy must still match `windowOpen` / `can.enterLive`, and must not claim socket enforcement that is not shipped.

## Forbidden

- A magic number or inline enum filter copied into a screen, hook, or modal when a mirror module exists.
- Copy that promises a rule the server does not enforce, or that stays silent about one it does — every note the UI renders must map to a real gate, and the condition that shows it must match the condition the server checks (same statuses, same window).
- Treating a mirror as authority: never skip the request, invent a client-side approval, or suppress a server denial because the mirror agreed.
